   "form": "By a sign asked",
   "line": "A sign sought and given — Gideon's fleece; Hezekiah's shadow. And a sign offered to Ahaz, and refused.",
   "decided": "Whether the word spoken would stand. Gideon asked whether God would save Israel by his hand (Judges 6:36) — the fleece fed in by hand, below. Hezekiah asked for a sign that he would live and go up to the house of the LORD, and the shadow went backward ten degrees (2 Kings 20:8–11; Isaiah 38:7–8). Ahaz was offered a sign and refused to ask (Isaiah 7:11–14) — and a sign was given anyway.",
   "fed": {
    "title": "Fed in by hand — the wet fleece (Judges 6:36–40)",
    "text": "Put into this section by hand (2026-11-24): “Wet fleece was a sign for someone.” Gideon laid a fleece of wool on the floor. First night: dew on the fleece only, and the ground dry — he wrung a bowl full of water out of it. Second night: the fleece dry, and dew on all the ground. What it settled: whether God would save Israel by Gideon's hand, as He had said (Judges 6:36; the call and the promise at 6:14–16). Gideon knew he was asking again — “Let not thine anger be hot against me, and I will speak but this once” (6:39). The outcome stands in Judges 7: the battle won with three hundred men."
